## Recommended refactor shape

### 1. Split by dependency direction, not by visual intuition

**Claim**: Keep `main.cpp` as the composition/root layer; move reusable rendering into pure functions that return `ftxui::Element`; move theme helpers into a lower-level module; move generic utilities into modules that do **not** include FTXUI unless they truly need it.

**Evidence** ([FTXUI DOM docs](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/doc/module-dom.md#L6-L20)):

```md
This module defines a hierarchical set of `ftxui::Element`. An element manages
the layout and can be responsive to the terminal dimension changes.

...

The list of all elements are included and can be accessed by including the
corresponding header file:
#include <ftxui/dom/elements.hpp>
```

**Explanation**: Pane renderers are naturally pure DOM builders: given state/view-model input, return an `ftxui::Element`. That makes them easy to test by rendering to an offscreen `Screen` and comparing output, instead of requiring the interactive loop.

Recommended dependency layers:

```text
main.cpp
  └── app composition / event loop / component tree
        ├── panes/*.hpp, panes/*.cpp       // pure or near-pure ftxui::Element builders
        │     └── theme.hpp
        ├── theme.hpp, theme.cpp           // colors, decorators, style constants
        └── util/*.hpp, util/*.cpp         // string/math/domain helpers, preferably no FTXUI
```

Avoid this:

```text
theme.hpp -> panes.hpp -> app_state.hpp -> main-owned globals
panes.hpp -> main.hpp
util.hpp  -> ftxui/component/component.hpp unnecessarily
```

Prefer this:

```text
util/string.hpp              // no FTXUI
theme/theme.hpp              // may include ftxui/dom/elements.hpp or ftxui/screen/color.hpp
panes/foo_pane.hpp           // includes only what its signature requires
main.cpp                     // includes everything and wires it together
```

---

### 2. Use minimal FTXUI includes per module

**Claim**: For pure pane renderers, include DOM headers, not the whole component stack, unless the pane actually constructs interactive components.

**Evidence** ([`elements.hpp`](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/include/ftxui/dom/elements.hpp#L7-L25)):

```cpp
#include <functional>
#include <memory>

#include <string_view>
#include "ftxui/dom/canvas.hpp"
#include "ftxui/dom/direction.hpp"
#include "ftxui/dom/flexbox_config.hpp"
#include "ftxui/dom/linear_gradient.hpp"
#include "ftxui/dom/node.hpp"
#include "ftxui/screen/box.hpp"
#include "ftxui/screen/color.hpp"
#include "ftxui/screen/terminal.hpp"
#include "ftxui/util/export.hpp"
#include "ftxui/util/ref.hpp"

namespace ftxui {
class Node;
using Element = std::shared_ptr<Node>;
using Elements = std::vector<Element>;
```

**Explanation**: `ftxui::Element` and layout/style helpers live in the DOM layer. A header declaring `ftxui::Element RenderFooPane(...)` usually only needs `<ftxui/dom/elements.hpp>` or, if you can avoid exposing FTXUI in the header, a source-only include.

Practical rule:

```cpp
// foo_pane.hpp
#pragma once

#include <ftxui/dom/elements.hpp>  // if the public signature returns ftxui::Element

struct FooPaneViewModel;

ftxui::Element RenderFooPane(const FooPaneViewModel& vm);
```

If a helper only returns a color or decorator:

```cpp
// theme.hpp
#pragma once

#include <ftxui/dom/elements.hpp>
#include <ftxui/screen/color.hpp>

namespace theme {
ftxui::Decorator Muted();
ftxui::Decorator Accent();
ftxui::Color ErrorColor();
}
```

Do **not** include `<ftxui/component/component.hpp>` from pure pane headers unless the header exposes `ftxui::Component`.

---

### 3. Keep interactive `Component` construction separate from pure `Element` rendering

**Claim**: FTXUI distinguishes single-frame `Element` rendering from dynamic interactive `Component` behavior.

**Evidence** ([component docs](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/doc/module-component.md#L11-L22)):

```md
A `ftxui::App` defines a main loop that renders a component.

A `ftxui::Component` is a shared pointer to a `ftxui::ComponentBase`. The latter defines:
  - `ftxui::ComponentBase::Render()`: How to render the interface.
  - `ftxui::ComponentBase::OnEvent()`: How to react to events.
  - `ftxui::ComponentBase::Add()`: Construct a parent/child relationship
    between two components. The tree of components is used to define how to
    navigate using the keyboard.

`ftxui::Element` are used to render a single frame.

`ftxui::Component` are used to render dynamic user interface, producing multiple
frame, and updating its state on events.
```

**Explanation**: This is the key architectural split. Put pure visual layout into functions like:

```cpp
ftxui::Element RenderStatusPane(const StatusPaneViewModel&);
ftxui::Element RenderInventoryPane(const InventoryPaneViewModel&);
ftxui::Element RenderCommandPane(const CommandPaneViewModel&);
```

Keep input handling and state mutation in component construction/composition:

```cpp
ftxui::Component MakeStatusComponent(AppState&);
ftxui::Component MakeInventoryComponent(AppState&);
```

Or, even cleaner:

```cpp
// Pure:
ftxui::Element RenderInventoryPane(const InventoryPaneViewModel&);

// Interactive adapter:
ftxui::Component MakeInventoryPaneComponent(AppState&);
```

This prevents the render functions from depending on `ScreenInteractive`, event loops, or global mutable state.

---

### 4. Treat `Renderer` lambdas as adapters around pure functions

**Claim**: FTXUI’s `Renderer` is designed to bind a render function into the component tree.

**Evidence** ([`component.hpp`](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/include/ftxui/component/component.hpp#L145-L151)):

```cpp
FTXUI_EXPORT(COMPONENT)
Component Renderer(Component child, std::function<Element()>);
FTXUI_EXPORT(COMPONENT) Component Renderer(std::function<Element()>);
FTXUI_EXPORT(COMPONENT)
Component Renderer(std::function<Element(bool /* focused */)>);
FTXUI_EXPORT(COMPONENT) ComponentDecorator Renderer(ElementDecorator);
```

**Explanation**: Instead of moving giant `Renderer([&] { ... })` bodies into another file unchanged, extract the body into a pure function, then keep a tiny adapter lambda near the component wiring:

```cpp
auto inventory_renderer = ftxui::Renderer([&] {
  return RenderInventoryPane(BuildInventoryViewModel(state));
});
```

That preserves behavior because the capture semantics remain visible in composition code, while the layout becomes testable.

