## TDD-oriented verification

### 1. Golden render tests for pure panes

**Claim**: FTXUI supports deterministic offscreen rendering into `Screen`, and its own tests compare rendered output strings.

**Evidence** ([selection test](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/src/ftxui/dom/selection_test.cpp#L149-L158)):

```cpp
auto screen = App::FixedSize(20, 2);

Selection selection(2, 0, 2, 1);
Render(screen, element.get(), selection);

EXPECT_EQ(selection.GetParts(), "rem ipsum dolor\nUt ");
EXPECT_EQ(screen.ToString(),
          "Lo\x1B[7mrem ipsum dolor\x1B[27m   \r\n"
          "\x1B[7mUt \x1B[27menim ad minim    ");
```

**Explanation**: This is the model to copy for panes: construct an `Element`, render it to a fixed-size screen, compare `screen.ToString()` to an expected string.

Example test shape:

```cpp
TEST(StatusPaneTest, RendersHealthyPlayer) {
  auto element = RenderStatusPane({
    .name = "Harry",
    .health = 10,
    .max_health = 10,
    .focused = false,
  });

  auto screen = ftxui::Screen::Create(
      ftxui::Dimension::Fixed(40),
      ftxui::Dimension::Fixed(5));

  ftxui::Render(screen, element);

  EXPECT_EQ(screen.ToString(), expected);
}
```

### 2. Characterization tests before extraction

Before moving code, add tests around the current behavior:

- fixed terminal width/height;
- representative normal state;
- empty/minimal state;
- long text wrapping/truncation state;
- focused/selected state;
- error/warning color state if ANSI output is expected.

Then extract and require tests to remain unchanged.

### 3. Component tests only where events matter

**Claim**: FTXUI tests interactive behavior by constructing a component, creating a fixed-size app/screen, posting events, running the loop once, and asserting state/output.

**Evidence** ([selection test](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/src/ftxui/dom/selection_test.cpp#L61-L70)):

```cpp
TEST(SelectionTest, DefaultSelection) {
  auto component = Renderer([&] { return text("Lorem ipsum dolor"); });
  auto screen = App::FixedSize(20, 1);
  EXPECT_EQ(screen.GetSelection(), "");
  Loop loop(&screen, component);
  screen.PostEvent(MousePressed(3, 1));
  screen.PostEvent(MouseReleased(10, 1));
  loop.RunOnce();

  EXPECT_EQ(screen.GetSelection(), "rem ipsu");
}
```

**Explanation**: Use component-level tests sparingly for keyboard/mouse behavior. For simple pane layout, prefer pure render tests. For focus, selection, command input, or tab switching, test the `Component` adapter.

